# SecurityExpert — non-interactive runtime configuration (DEV.2.1)
#
# Reference only. Nothing in the codebase auto-loads this file; the container
# runtime injects the environment (Compose `env_file:` / `environment:`,
# Kubernetes `envFrom`). Copy to `.env` for local shell use if you want, but
# `.env` is gitignored and never read automatically.
#
# Per value, precedence is:  <VAR>_FILE  >  <VAR>  >  interactive prompt (TTY only)
# When stdin is not a TTY and a required value is unresolved, the process exits
# immediately naming the missing variable(s) — it never hangs on a prompt.
#
# The `<VAR>_FILE` variant points at a file whose (stripped) contents are the
# value — the Docker / Kubernetes secret-mount convention. Prefer it for the
# secret.

# Login / principal for CP SSH and PAN API auth
SECURITYEXPERT_PRINCIPAL=
# SECURITYEXPERT_PRINCIPAL_FILE=/run/secrets/securityexpert_principal

# Authentication secret (password / API secret)
SECURITYEXPERT_SECRET=
# SECURITYEXPERT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/securityexpert_secret

# Check Point Management (MDS/SMC) endpoint — hostname or IP.
# Required for --only cp / vsx / all and the --cp-config-* modes.
SECURITYEXPERT_CP_MDS_ENDPOINT=
# SECURITYEXPERT_CP_MDS_ENDPOINT_FILE=/run/secrets/securityexpert_cp_mds_endpoint

# Palo Alto Panorama endpoint — hostname or IP.
# Required for --only panorama / pan-config / all.
SECURITYEXPERT_PANORAMA_ENDPOINT=
# SECURITYEXPERT_PANORAMA_ENDPOINT_FILE=/run/secrets/securityexpert_panorama_endpoint

# External runtime root (state/output/logs live here, never in the repo).
# On Windows it defaults to %LOCALAPPDATA%\SecurityExpert\runtime; on macOS/
# Linux it defaults to $XDG_DATA_HOME/SecurityExpert/runtime (else
# $HOME/.local/share/SecurityExpert/runtime) -- a local dev convenience only.
# A container MUST set this explicitly to its mounted runtime volume (see
# docker-compose.yml) rather than rely on that default.
SECURITYEXPERT_RUNTIME_ROOT=/runtime

# RB.1 recovery-plane store (docs/design/BACKUP_RECOVERY_CONTRACTS.md §2).
# Mandatory and absolute -- there is NO default here, unlike RUNTIME_ROOT
# above: a recovery volume silently defaulting into place would reproduce
# the "assumed we had backups" failure the design exists to prevent. Must be
# a DIFFERENT volume from SECURITYEXPERT_RUNTIME_ROOT (docker-compose.yml
# already provisions securityexpert-recovery as a separate named volume,
# mounted only on worker, never on nginx). Nothing writes to it yet --
# RB.2/RB.3 collection is gated behind the network-device command gate.
SECURITYEXPERT_RECOVERY_ROOT=/recovery

# Optional: override the recovery-plane vault master key instead of letting
# one be generated on first use at <data_root>/.recovery_vault.key (mirrors
# DEV.2.2's SUPPORT_KEY pattern). Hex-encoded 32 bytes. Prefer leaving this
# unset in normal operation -- the auto-generated, persistent-volume key is
# the intended default.
# SECURITYEXPERT_RECOVERY_VAULT_KEY=

# --- Optional: existing per-path / hardening variables (unchanged by DEV.2.1) ---
# SECURITYEXPERT_CP_CONFIG_SSH_USERNAME=
# SECURITYEXPERT_CP_CONFIG_SSH_PASSWORD=

# --- Production trust hardening (DEV.2.2) ---
# Opt-in here; on the DEPLOY.1 server these move to mounted-and-required via
# docker-compose.prod.yml (deploy/secrets/README.md), not just an env flag --
# the strict host-key check fails closed with no usable host keys, and the
# CA bundle preflight fails closed if the path is not readable.
# SECURITYEXPERT_CP_MDS_STRICT_HOST_KEY=1
# SECURITYEXPERT_PAN_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/corporate-ca.pem

# Verify the above (and that data/.support_hmac.key already exists on the
# persistent volume from a prior run) with:
#   python main.py --persistent-secret-material-check

# --- Optional: DEV.3.2 distributed per-endpoint lock (cross-process only) ---
# Default (unset / 'memory') is the unchanged single-process 0.6.1C coordinator.
# 'postgres' requires the SECURITYEXPERT_COORDINATOR_POSTGRES_DSN below, the
# optional requirements-postgres.txt dependency, and a direct or session-mode
# (never transaction-mode) connection path — see
# docs/history/phase/DEV3_2_DISTRIBUTED_ENDPOINT_LOCK.md.
# SECURITYEXPERT_COORDINATOR_BACKEND=postgres
# SECURITYEXPERT_COORDINATOR_POSTGRES_DSN=postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/dbname

# --- Optional: DEV.3.3 distributed evidence store (multi-container only) ---
# Default (unset / 'filesystem') keeps the CAS metadata index, run manifests,
# last-known-good and scheduler state as per-container local files — unchanged
# behavior. 'postgres' moves those four to a shared PostgreSQL instance so
# several worker containers see one consistent evidence plane. Content-
# addressed payload blobs always stay on the runtime volume, either way.
# Requires the optional requirements-postgres.txt dependency. Unlike the
# coordinator backend below/above, this one holds no session-level locks, so a
# transaction-pooling proxy is safe here.
#
# IMPORTANT — these two knobs are independent and do not imply each other:
#   * SECURITYEXPERT_EVIDENCE_BACKEND=postgres gives every container the SAME
#     scheduler state, but NOT mutual exclusion over it.
#   * SECURITYEXPERT_COORDINATOR_BACKEND=postgres (DEV.3.2) is what stops two
#     schedulers dispatching the same due workflow in one interval.
# A multi-container deployment that schedules collection wants both.
#
# Run every container in a fleet on the SAME evidence backend: a mixed fleet
# silently fragments evidence across two stores with no error.
#
# The Postgres instance becomes an identity-bearing CLASS 2 asset (device
# names and management IPs are stored in full) — see
# PRIVACY_AND_DATA_HANDLING.md "Distributed evidence store" and
# docs/history/phase/DEV3_3_DISTRIBUTED_EVIDENCE_STORE_MIGRATION.md (E1).
# SECURITYEXPERT_EVIDENCE_BACKEND=postgres
# SECURITYEXPERT_EVIDENCE_POSTGRES_DSN=postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/dbname
